<Base title="Searched"
      eyebrow="Everything looked at, including everything that led nowhere"
      dek="An archive that publishes only its finds is advertising. This page lists every search made, what it returned, and what it cost — so that no one repeats a dead end, and so a null result can be read as evidence rather than silence.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Why a page of failures</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Most genealogical work is negative. Knowing that a register has been read line by line and does
      <em>not</em> contain a name is worth as much as finding one — provided somebody wrote it down. Nobody
      ever does, which is why the same fruitless afternoon gets spent again a decade later.</p>
    <p>So: everything below was actually looked at. Where a search failed, the reason is given — not there,
      not indexed, not digitised, restricted, or blocked. The distinction matters. A record that does not
      exist and a record nobody has scanned are different problems with different solutions.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The army enrolment drawer — the one that worked</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:1.2rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0"><span class="chip doc">Found</span> <strong>10 September 2026.</strong>
      Image group #112342450, <em>Fichas de enrolamiento</em>, Cajón 985 (Lera).
      Image 665 is <strong>Pablo Armando Lerena&rsquo;s own enrolment card</strong>, naming his father and
      mother. <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>The card →</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>It belongs on this page as much as on any other, because <strong>this archive had already found it
      and thrown it away.</strong> The machine index rendered him <em>Armand Lorena</em>, born at
      <em>Rosario de Lerma, Salta</em>, and the previous round wrote him off on four objections. Three were
      artefacts of the transcription. The image settled it in two minutes.</p>
    <p>Images <strong>650–688</strong> of the same drawer were then swept through the viewer&rsquo;s index
      panel. Five other Lerena families are in there — Gilberto, Alejandro, Arturo, Ramón, Carlos — and
      <strong>none is from Rosario de Santa Fe, and none has a father called Juan Carlos</strong>. No
      brother. That negative is recorded on the card&rsquo;s own page, with what it does and does not
      prove.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <strong>The lesson, taken to heart.</strong> An index entry is a pointer, not a fact. Everywhere this
    archive has excluded a candidate on indexed detail <em>alone</em>, the exclusion is provisional until
    the image is opened. Those cases are being re-checked.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The register itself, act by act</h2>
  <p class="dek">10 September 2026. Not the index &mdash; the book.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p>Every null this archive holds for Rosario has rested on a <strong>typescript index</strong>: a later
      compilation, made from the registers, listing surname, forename, year and folio. It has been read
      exhaustively. But an index is somebody&rsquo;s transcription, and <strong>whether the typist missed
      anything had never been tested</strong>.</p>
    <p>So <strong>Libro 24 was read act by act</strong>. Each opening carries about eight acts, and every
      act has the child&rsquo;s name written in the margin. All 183 margins in the span were read &mdash;
      not skimmed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:22px 0">
    <p style="margin:0;font-size:1.05rem"><strong>From 1 February to 15 March 1882 &mdash; acts 113 to 295,
      pages 390 to 435 &mdash; there is no Lerena.</strong><br />
    <span class="cap">He was born on 22 February 1882. The span covers three weeks before his birth and
    three weeks after it, with nothing skipped.</span></p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>What the book gave up along the way.</strong> Rosario cathedral baptised <strong>112
      children in January 1882 and 122 in February</strong> &mdash; about four a day. That is the first
      time this archive has had a rate, and it is what makes the sweep bounded rather than open-ended.</p>
    <p>And a filming detail worth knowing: page 425 has a paper slip pasted over it, an annotation dated
      <strong>29 May 1905</strong>, which on the film hides four acts. <strong>The next frame is the same
      page refilmed with the slip lifted</strong>, so nothing is lost. Whoever filmed this in 1974 was
      careful.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <strong>What it settles, and what it does not.</strong> The index is not hiding him &mdash; at least
    not here. For six weeks around his birth the register says exactly what the index says, and the two
    agree. That removes the most comfortable explanation: this archive can no longer say
    <em>&ldquo;perhaps the typist missed it.&rdquo;</em>
    <br /><br />
    <strong>But six weeks is not a year.</strong> Infants were usually baptised within days or weeks,
    which is why that window was chosen &mdash; yet families did wait. A baptism in April, or in 1883, or
    at seven years old, is <em>not</em> excluded by this. What is established, from the book rather than
    from an index, is that <strong>he was not baptised at the cathedral in the six weeks around his
    birth</strong>. The rest of 1882 is about a hundred and thirty more frames: a long afternoon, not a
    mystery.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The marriage index &mdash; read again, at a resolution that leaves no doubt</h2>
  <p class="dek">10 September 2026. The enrolment card gave a father&rsquo;s name; this is where his
    wife&rsquo;s maiden name would be.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p>If <strong>Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> married at Rosario, the bride&rsquo;s surname is in the
      <em>&Iacute;ndice de matrimonios 1860&ndash;1904</em> &mdash; and that is the one thing the card could
      not settle. The registers had been swept before, but for a different question and at a resolution
      that had already produced one bad reading.</p>
    <p>Unlike the baptism index, <strong>this one is handwritten</strong>. At the zoom the viewer allows,
      the initials are illegible and the surnames merely guessable &mdash; <strong>which is exactly how
      &ldquo;Lavena&rdquo; became &ldquo;Larena&rdquo;</strong> in an earlier session and kept a false
      candidate alive for two days. Read at full tile resolution, the L band is unambiguous.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:22px 0">
    <p style="margin:0;font-size:1.05rem"><strong>No Lerena marriage at Rosario cathedral, 1867&ndash;1885.</strong><br />
    <span class="cap">Libro 6 through Libro 9 &mdash; the whole window in which a man whose son was born in
    February 1882 could have married.</span></p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The nearest approaches were checked at 1.4&times; to be certain: <strong>Lercari L.</strong> (1875),
      a Genoese name, and <strong>Llisalinos J.</strong> (1882). And the old error is now settled beyond
      argument &mdash; Libro 9, 1884, folio 20 reads <strong>LAVENA V. &times; BELMONTE A.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The L section, read end to end and closed</h2>
  <p class="dek">10 September 2026. The last open corner of the Rosario index, finished so it can be left
    behind.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p>The enrolment card changed the question. The earlier sweeps looked for the forename
      <em>Pablo Armando</em> under any surname. The card gives a father called <strong>Lerena</strong>, so
      the question became: <strong>does the surname appear in Rosario at all, in any year?</strong> Against
      that question the record had gaps &mdash; nothing recorded for 1879, 1880 or 1896&ndash;1900, and Ll
      read only for 1880&ndash;1884.</p>
    <p><strong>Every opening of the L section and of the Ll section has now been read, first to last, in
      order</strong> &mdash; 41 frames, 38 of them distinct and 3 duplicate exposures, each verified on
      arrival by year and Libro/folio, running continuously from <strong>Libro 23 folio 145</strong> to
      <strong>Libro 48 folio 546</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:22px 0">
    <p style="margin:0;font-size:1.05rem"><strong>No Lerena. No Llerena. No Larena. No Lorena.</strong><br />
    <span class="cap">Not in any year from 1879 to 1901. Not in either section.</span></p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The L section opens at <strong>1880</strong> &mdash; there are no L entries at all for 1879 &mdash;
      and runs past 1900 into 1901. The entire Ll section, across twenty-two years, comes to
      <strong>twenty-six entries</strong>. The nearest thing to the name in either section is
      <strong>LLERA</strong>: <em>Pedro</em>, 1898, and <em>Ida C.</em>, 1900.</p>
    <p><strong>And the Armandos.</strong> The forename this archive has argued is rare does exist at
      Rosario &mdash; four times in the L band, and never in the right year:
      <strong>Lofré</strong> (1889), <strong>Luján</strong> (1895), <strong>Lunardi</strong> (1895),
      <strong>Limonetti</strong> (1897). All after 1889. <em>Armando</em> looks like a name that came into
      fashion at Rosario in the 1890s &mdash; which makes a boy christened Pablo <em>Armando</em> in
      February 1882 slightly unusual for his date, and slightly more likely to have been named for
      someone.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <strong>The method, which is the part worth keeping.</strong> The index is <em>typewritten</em>, not
    handwritten &mdash; the obstacle was never the hand, it was the viewer, whose zoom would not go far
    enough to read a page. FamilySearch serves each page as <strong>DeepZoom tiles</strong>, and level 13
    for this film is <strong>20 &times; 15 tiles of 257 px</strong> &mdash; about 5100 &times; 3800 for a
    two-page opening. Assembling those tiles into a plain HTML grid at 42% puts a whole opening on one
    screen with every surname legible. Image <em>numbers</em> drift between viewer states and are not to be
    trusted; the image <strong>ark</strong> is stable, and that is what the data file records.
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><strong>What it settles, and what it does not.</strong> It settles that the surname is not in the
      Rosario cathedral index &mdash; absent, across twenty-two years, in a section read end to end. Until
      1888 the cathedral was the city&rsquo;s only parish. It does <em>not</em> settle where he was
      baptised. Three possibilities remain, and this archive should stop calling it a null and start
      calling it a question: he was not baptised as an infant; he was baptised outside the cathedral&rsquo;s
      books; or <strong>the index itself is incomplete</strong> &mdash; it is a later typescript compiled
      from the registers, not the registers. That last is the one nobody likes to say aloud, and it is why
      an <em>act-by-act</em> reading of the 1882 register is still worth doing one day.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Rosario — the baptism registers</h2>
  <p class="dek">Nuestra Señora del Rosario, the cathedral parish. Alphabetical index, DGS 4530325,
    read by eye at the film.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Covered</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Surname LERENA</strong>, every year</td><td class="m">1882–1895</td>
          <td>Nothing. Fourteen consecutive years, line by line.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Surname <strong>LLERENA</strong> (the <em>Ll</em> section)</td><td class="m">1880–1884</td>
          <td>Nothing. The whole Ll section for those five years is seven entries.</td></tr>
        <tr><td colspan="3" style="padding-top:18px"><strong>The 1882 sweep — every surname, looking for the
          given name <em>Pablo Armando</em></strong>, on the theory that a child born outside marriage was
          registered under his mother's name</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Letters <strong>A B C D E F G H I J K L Ll M N O P Q R S T U V W Y Z</strong></td>
          <td class="m">1882</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Complete</span> <strong>Every section of the index, read.
            He is not there.</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Letter <strong>X</strong></td><td class="m">—</td>
          <td>There is no X section. The index runs W straight to Y.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Letters <strong>K</strong> and <strong>Y</strong></td><td class="m">1882</td>
          <td>Contain no 1882 entries at all — K jumps 1881 to 1883; the whole Y section is one line
            for 1900.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>Eleven near misses, every one excluded.</strong>
    <em>Chaparro · Pablo H.</em> (f.67) · <em>Funes · Armando</em> (f.163) ·
    <em>Eduardo · Paul (N.)</em> (f.505) · <em>Gorosito · Pablo</em> (1881 f.71) · a <em>Pablo J.</em>
    among the G's · a <em>Pablo</em> among the I's (1884) · <em>Pablo Eduardo</em> among the O's (f.555) ·
    a surname-only <em>Pablo · (N.)</em> among the P's · <em>Quevedo · Juan B. Pablo</em> (f.427) ·
    <em>Villarreal · Pablo del R.</em> (f.559) · and the closest of all,
    <strong><em>Ronillon · Armando Juan</em></strong> (f.498). None is him — but eleven near hits across
    718 images show the sweep catches the name when it is there, which is what makes the null result
    mean something.</div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">Useful incidental discovery: the index marks illegitimate births
    <strong>(N.)</strong>, for <em>natural</em>. And <strong>Expósito</strong> — the surname given to
    foundlings — is itself a heading in the E section, which was read.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The neighbouring years, 1881 and 1883 — also complete</h3>
  <p class="prose">The passport&rsquo;s <strong>22 February 1882</strong> is the only authority for the
    year, and it was written on a document issued fifty-eight years after the fact. So the sweep was run
    again across the two years either side. <strong>All twenty-six sections are now read for 1881, 1882 and
    1883 alike. He is in none of them.</strong> Roughly two hundred image-reads across a 718-image film.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>1881</th><th>1883</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>A – J</strong></td><td>Complete</td><td>Complete</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>K</strong></td><td>Four entries in the whole year</td><td>Two entries</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>L</strong></td><td>Complete — no Lerena</td><td>Complete — no Lerena</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ll</strong></td><td><strong>No 1881 entries at all</strong></td>
          <td>Three entries — no Llerena</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>M – P</strong></td><td>Complete</td><td>Complete</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Q</strong></td><td><strong>Ten entries in the whole year</strong></td>
          <td>Thirteen entries</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>R – V</strong></td><td>Complete</td><td>Complete</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>W</strong></td><td><strong>One entry</strong> — Williams, Josefa Aurelia</td>
          <td><strong>One entry</strong> — Windels, María Elisa</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>X</strong></td><td colspan="2">There is no X section in this index.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Y</strong></td><td colspan="2">The entire section is one line, for 1900.
          No entries in either year.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Z</strong></td><td>Complete</td><td>Complete</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>Twenty-odd near misses across the three years, every
    one excluded.</strong> Only <strong>two Armandos</strong> exist in all three years —
    <em>Ronillon · Armando Juan</em> and <em>Funes · Armando</em>, both 1882. The Pablos are many and none
    fits: Chaparro Pablo H. · Gorosito Pablo · Carara Pablo · López Pablo · Lamberti Atilio Pablo Juan ·
    Morón Juan Pablo · Días José Pablo · Ledesma Juan Pablo · Leguisamón Pedro Pablo · López Pablo
    Gregorio · Luraschi Santos Pablo · Medina Pablo Mauricio · Mendieta José Pablo · Olivieri Juan Pablo ·
    Olguín Pablo Fabio A. · Ojeda Pablo Eduardo · Pantaro Juan Pablo · Quevedo Juan B. Pablo · Tello Pedro
    Paulino · Velez Juan Pablo · Villarreal Pablo del R. · Zárate Pablo Jorge, and bare <em>Pablo</em>
    entries in G, H and I. <strong>That is what makes the null result mean something</strong> — the method
    plainly catches the name whenever it is present.</div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">A practical discovery, recorded so it is not tripped over again:
    <strong>some frames of this film are duplicates of their neighbour</strong> — image 418 returns 417,
    424 returns 423 — which is also the likeliest explanation of the 718-versus-655 image count between the
    two viewer states. Coverage is therefore verified by <strong>folio continuity</strong>, checking that
    each opening&rsquo;s last folio runs unbroken into the next opening&rsquo;s first, and never by image
    number alone.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">What the null result proves — and what it does not</h3>
  <p class="prose">The cathedral was the <strong>only parish in Rosario until 1888</strong>, and civil
    registration in Santa Fe did not begin until about 1886. So this index was the single most likely
    record of his birth anywhere. It rules out one specific thing, cleanly: <strong>no child named
    Pablo Armando was baptised at Rosario cathedral in 1882, under any surname at all.</strong></p>
  <p class="prose">It does not rule out four other possibilities, and it is worth being exact about them.
    He may have been baptised under a <strong>different given name</strong> — he was <em>Robert Paul</em>
    in South Africa and <em>Pablo Armando</em> only on the passport, and the sweep looked for that pair.
    He may have been baptised in a <strong>different year</strong>, if the passport's 22 February 1882 was
    wrong or approximate. He may have been born <strong>somewhere else entirely</strong> and only later
    associated with Rosario. Or he may never have been entered in this register at all.</p>
  <p class="prose"><strong>The second of those has now been tested and closed: 1881 and 1883 are read in
    full, and he is in neither.</strong> So the Rosario baptism route is <em>exhausted</em>, not merely
    unsuccessful, and it should not be reopened without a new reason. What remains open is the first, third
    and fourth &mdash; a different given name, a different place, or no baptism recorded here at all.</p>
  <p class="prose">The weight now falls on evidence gathered elsewhere, which grew stronger while this
    sweep ran: that <strong>he held Argentine nationality by 1911</strong>, proved from outside the family;
    that the surname&rsquo;s kindred sits at <strong>Trinidad, in Flores, Uruguay</strong>, where
    <strong>civil registration began in 1879</strong> &mdash; three years before his stated birth; and that
    the family&rsquo;s political-refugee story, if it means expulsion under the <em>Ley de Residencia</em>,
    would require him to have been <strong>foreign-born</strong>, which would explain this null result
    completely. <strong>The cheapest untried test is now Uruguayan civil registration for 1882</strong>,
    not another parish register.</p>
</section>

