<section class="blk">
  <h2>Spain</h2>
  <p class="dek">FamilySearch: Spain Baptisms 1502–1940, Catholic Church Records 1307–2005, Civil Records
    1475–2005, and the provincial municipal registers.</p>
  <p class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">The last of the four candidate birthplaces, and it had a real
    claim: <strong>888 Lerenas in Spain</strong> — the largest absolute total anywhere — and the CEMLA
    arrivals cluster hard on <strong>Logroño, La Rioja</strong>.</p>
  <p class="prose"><strong>The whole of Spain yields one Pablo Lerena of the right generation:</strong>
    <em>Pablo Timoteo Lerena Muñoz</em>, born <strong>1878</strong> at Bochones, Atienza, Guadalajara, son
    of Pedro Lerena Pérez and Baltasara Muñoz Bermejos. Four years early — and he married at Atienza and
    stayed. He is not our man.</p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>But the useful result is the other one.
    There is no Armando Lerena in Spain at all.</strong> Not one, in any collection, in any century — every
    hit is fuzzy noise. And <strong>Armando is the name this family keeps</strong>: Pablo <em>Armando</em>;
    his son Roque Luis <em>Armando</em>; <em>Anton Armando</em> of the 1980 divorce and the 2013 estate; and
    <em>Gregorio Armando</em> Lerena, marrying at Flores in Uruguay in 1930.
    <br><br><strong>Armando is a Río de la Plata name, not a Spanish peninsular one of the 1880s.</strong>
    It entered Spanish usage through Italian influence — precisely the demographic signature of Buenos
    Aires, Rosario and Montevideo in the late nineteenth century, and not of rural La Rioja. Across three
    whole years of the Rosario index there were <strong>two</strong> Armandos: rare, but present. Across
    four centuries of indexed Spanish records, <strong>none</strong>.
    <br><br><strong>So the name points away from Spain and back across the Atlantic.</strong> That is not
    proof — but it is the first evidence in this project that discriminates <em>between</em> the candidate
    origins rather than merely failing to confirm one.</div>
  <p class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">The Spanish bearers are also <strong>scattered rather than a
    kindred</strong> — Almería, Écija, Barcelona, Valencia, Valladolid, Navarra, Guadalajara, Huelva, León,
    Ferrol — and in most of them <strong>Lerena is the <em>second</em>, maternal surname</strong>:
    <em>Manuel Fernández Lerena</em>, <em>Juan García Lerena</em>. It behaves like a name being absorbed
    into other families, not one founding them. Nothing like the six brothers at Trinidad.</p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>The honest limit:</strong> this is a weaker negative than
    the Uruguayan one. Spanish parish registers are enormous and only patchily indexed, and diocesan
    archives hold far more than has been keyed. An 1882 baptism in an unindexed Riojan parish would not
    surface here. What can fairly be said is that <strong>he is not in the indexed Spanish records, and the
    given name his family carried does not belong to Spain</strong>.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Uruguay — civil registration</h2>
  <p class="dek">FamilySearch: Uruguay Civil Registration 1879–2020, Catholic Church Records 1726–2000,
    Baptisms 1750–1900, and the Civil Registration Index Cards 1900–1937.</p>
  <p class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">This was the successor to the Rosario sweep, and it was chosen
    for a specific reason: <strong>Uruguayan civil registration began in 1879</strong> — three years before
    his stated birth, and <strong>seven years before Santa Fe&rsquo;s</strong>. The absence of early civil
    registration is exactly what makes the Argentine trail dead-end, and it does not apply here. If he was
    Uruguayan-born in 1882, there ought to be a civil birth record.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Every Pablo Lerena in Uruguay</th><th>Event</th><th>Place</th><th>Parents</th></tr></thead>
