  </div>
  <p class="prose" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Not one born in 1882. Not one named Armando.</strong>
    Nor is there a <em>Pablo Armando</em> of any surname born in Uruguay in 1882 — the nearest are
    <strong>Pablo Armando Gimenez</strong> (b. 15 January 1885, Soriano) and <strong>Pablo Armando
    Pilón</strong> (b. 1885, Dolores, Soriano), both three years late and neither a Lerena.</p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>The control that makes this negative worth something.</strong>
    A null is only as good as the coverage behind it — so: <strong>the index does hold Lerena births from
    exactly this window.</strong> María Luisa Lerena, born <strong>19 July 1882</strong>, Sección 1, Río
    Negro. Ema Justina Felicia Lerena, 18 May 1881, San José. Alberto Julián Lerena, 17 August 1883, San
    José. Carlos Augusto Federico Lerena, 9 July 1883, Montevideo. <strong>1882 Lerena births are
    indexed.</strong> This is not a hole in the coverage; it is an absence in the record.</div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">It remains an <em>index</em>, not the registers themselves.
    FamilySearch&rsquo;s Uruguayan coverage is not complete for every department and year, so an unindexed
    1882 birth in a thinly-covered department would not surface here. The honest statement is that
    <strong>he is not in the indexed Uruguayan civil registration</strong> — which substantially weakens the
    Uruguayan-birth idea without finally killing it.</p>
</section>

