<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the failures add up to</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Three independent bodies of evidence decline to place this family at Rosario: fourteen years of
      cathedral baptisms, the whole FamilySearch index, and the 1895 national census. And there is no civil
      fallback, because provincial registration did not yet exist.</p>
    <p>That is not nothing. It means <strong>his South American origin rests entirely on his own
      passport</strong> — a document he swore at a consulate, naming a city, a province and a day. The
      record has not contradicted him. It has simply, so far, refused to agree.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">If you have looked somewhere this page does not list, that is
    worth knowing too. Corrections and additions are the most valuable thing anyone can send.</div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Full-text search — swept, and it cannot reach him</h2>
  <p class="prose">Every null on this page is really the same null: <em>not in the index</em>. Almost all of
    this work has depended on somebody having keyed a record into a searchable database first.
    <strong>FamilySearch&rsquo;s full-text search reads the handwriting itself</strong>, in records nobody
    has indexed. It was listed here for two rounds as the clearest remaining opening. On
    <strong>10 September 2026 it was actually run</strong>, and the answer is structural rather than
    negative.</p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>The surname is in the corpus in quantity. The place
    is not.</strong> A sweep for <em>Lerena</em> returns, in order of weight:
    <strong>Arequipa, Peru</strong> — an entire Lerena population this archive had never met;
    <strong>Valladolid and Madrid</strong> nobility genealogies and notarial books;
    <strong>Trinidad, Flores</strong> and <strong>Montevideo</strong>; Jalisco, Mexico; and a scatter of
    American newspapers.
    <br><br><strong>Santa Fe is not in it. Rosario is not in it.</strong> Not thinly represented —
    absent. The instrument held in reserve for the hardest question does not cover the ground the
    question is on. An earlier note here recorded two Santa Fe <em>La Capital</em> marriage volumes as
    full-text searchable; they do not answer for Rosario, 170 km away, and no Rosario volume appears.</div>
  <p class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">A phrase search for <strong>&ldquo;Juan Carlos Lerena&rdquo;</strong>
    — the name the enrolment card had just produced — returns exactly two collections, both Montevideo,
    and both are one man: <strong>Juan Carlos González Lerena</strong>, dispensed from the three canonical
    banns on 8 November 1922 to marry Concepción Petrona Urbano. Born 1899. Not ours. The dispensation
    register was read rather than assumed.</p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">Recorded as a closed item rather than left open as a hope. Knowing
    an instrument cannot reach a place is worth more than continuing to list it as untried.</p>
</section>

