  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <strong>Which leaves the death notice, and it now carries a prediction.</strong> A South African death
    notice names the deceased&rsquo;s parents at question 19 — Roque&rsquo;s 1973 notice gave
    <em>Roberto Lerena</em> and <em>Mary Septima Lerena</em>. So the 1950 notice for
    <strong>Robert Paul Lerena</strong> should say <strong>Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> and
    <strong>María</strong>. That is a test, not a fishing trip: if it agrees, the card is confirmed by a
    second document written on the other side of the world in another language; if María&rsquo;s entry
    carries a <strong>maiden name</strong>, the one thing the card could not settle is settled in a line.
    The letter to the Western Cape Archives is written and waiting to go.
  </div>
</section>

